this performance with the pops  is probably one of the most exciting  ones i've done  it's my first time playing with a  professional orchestra  the focus of my phd is machine learning  and statistics  and i'm investigating theoretical  questions like how much data do you need  in order to actually make a good  decision  [Music]  it's great having music as an outlet to  just be able to do something in your  free time  and to just kind of switch your mind out  of research and focus on something  different and activate different areas  of your brain  i think my younger self will be elated  to hear that i was playing with the pops  because  i would always like watch a youtube  recording of like their  stars and stripes forever performance  and i was always inspired by that i just  watched every fourth of july so  when you come into the practice rooms at  mit you're supposed to  disinfect the keys before and after you  practice  and then it just makes a sound like it  sounds like this  so you just hear that in the practice  rooms a lot when people are coming in  and leaving to practice  [Music]  i gave a recital recently that  had just a few members of the live  audience i had to wear a mask when i was  performing  the rest of the people watched on zoom  so it was nice because i could have  my friends in california watch  so i chose to play the romanov concerto  because first of all it's a great piece  of music  and it's just also very close to my  heart because i've been  playing it for quite a while i learned  it four years ago  i do like the first movement for it's  beautiful lyrical things but  there's some also deeper angst i guess  the solo sections of this piece are very  intimate and  there's a lot of beauty in them they're  very  passionate and loving but it's also  kind of sad and bittersweet  i guess in a time pandemic sometimes you  don't want to plunge into something  completely new  maybe different people have different  ways of coping but i like to go back to  what i  originally liked and rediscover what i  liked about it  i think one of the most important things  about music is that you can kind of take  away what you want to take away from it  there's a lot of interpretive history  the different people play different ways  i hope that my particular way of playing  resonates in you and somehow  brings you joy  you
 

